input.visionGap:
There is NO quantified state primitive anywhere in the engine. AgentState is entirely symbolic/social (alive, location, mood, status, beliefs, relationships) — confirmed: no balance, no inventory, no HP, no standing, no faction. The only numeric-with-modifiers pattern that exists is MoodModifier. Every second genre the vision names is gated on this same missing shape: Economy needs gold+inventory, Combat needs HP+damage, Politics needs influence+standing, Survival needs need-meters — all are 'a named, clamped, decaying scalar with modifiers and a condition op to read it.' Until the engine has a generic scalar-attribute (and item-transfer) layer, a 2nd genre can only fake quantitative state by stuffing numbers into the untyped event.payload and reimplementing accounting in tick handlers — which violates conservation, has no persistence integrity, and is invisible to CG conditions and affordance scoring. The INV-14 vocabulary leaks (crime-named AffordanceRequirement fields, hardcoded investigator/canSentence branches, CrimeScene in the public API) are real and worth fixing, but they are TYPE-LEVEL smells the registry escape hatches already route around at runtime. The QUANTITATIVE-STATE gap is a hard capability ceiling, not a smell: it is the single thing standing between 'a different crime town' and 'a different game.'",
  "recommendation": {
    "title": "Ship a minimal Economy spike FIRST, then build the generic scalar-attribute subsystem it exposes",
    "rationale": "Two of the maps independently converge on the same conclusion: the quantified-resource primitive is the #1 missing capability for a 2nd genre, and a thin 2nd genre reveals more locked assumptions than any amount of analysis. Doing them in this order is the highest-leverage move. The Economy spike (M) is cheap and immediately empirical: it will hit the exact four documented blockers (no resource state, beliefConfidence-only requirement params, hardcoded capability flags, pre-existing ShopItem/TradeIntent/EmotionalPressure-union collisions) and turn the maps' predictions into a concrete, ranked engine-edit list grounded in a running module rather than speculation. Then the scalar-attribute subsystem (L) — built by GENERALIZING the MoodModifier pattern the engine already proves — is the one primitive that simultaneously backs Economy gold, Combat HP, Politics standing, and Survival needs. That single subsystem plus the requirement/capability generalization it forces is what converts the engine from 'crime-content-swappable' to 'genre-extensible,' which is exactly the user's stated goal of widening the game-creation space rather than closing the crime game.",
    "tradeoff": "The spike's output is intentionally a throwaway/fake economy (numbers in payload, no conservation), which sits in tension with the user's 'build complete systems, no scaffolds' rule — so it must be explicitly framed and time-boxed as a generality PROBE whose deliverable is the blocker list, not a shipped feature, and its fake-state must be torn out and replaced by the real scalar subsystem. The cost is doing the Economy affordances/events twice (once thin against payload, once properly against the new primitive). The alternative — building the scalar subsystem first with no module driving it — risks designing the wrong shape in the abstract and over-engineering transactions/ledgers nobody is exercising yet. Accept the rework as the price of designing the primitive against real, observed module demand.",
    "firstConcreteStep": "Author a ~80-line minimal Economy module (mirroring social/index.ts, the lean template) registering ONLY: registerEventCategory('economy',['TRADE','FRAUD']) + registerEventLabels + registerEventTypeFlags (FRAUD transferable so beliefs gossip); two AffordanceDefinitions (defraud: high-O/low-A weights, resistTraits honesty:high; trade: cooperative weights) pushed into AFFORDANCE_REGISTRY + registerActionVerbs; one ConsequenceRule FRAUD→REPUTATION_LOSS gated on a trait condition; and a registerStepHandler('attempt_fraud') that today just emits the event. Wire it in server/index.ts alongside registerCrimeJusticeModule. Run it in the existing crime world via the debug API and record EVERY point where you are forced to either edit the engine or stuff state into event.payload. That recorded list — expected to surface the scalar-state gap, the beliefConfidence-only requirement-param limit (al.ts:294), the hardcoded capability branches (al.ts:332-335), and the ShopItem/TradeIntent collision — becomes the precise, prioritized spec for the scalar-attribute subsystem and the requirement/capability hardening that follow."
  },
  "summary": "I verified the four maps against the live code: INV-14's import boundary is genuinely clean (zero module imports in engine), the AffordanceRequirement crime-vocabulary leak and hardcoded investigator/canSentence branches are real (al.ts:11-49, 332-335), there is NO quantified state anywhere in AgentState (only a dead-end static shop list), CrimeScene is hardcoded in the engine's public API, and MoodModifier is the one existing named-scalar-with-modifiers pattern. The maps are accurate and internally consistent. The engine is ~80-90% genre-extensible at the registry/data plane (events, affordances, scoring, steps, consequences, beliefs, conversation, evidence, lifecycle hooks are all genuinely registry-driven), but it hits a HARD ceiling on quantitative simulation: no resource/inventory/HP/standing/faction state exists, so no second genre can model conserved value, damage, or collective power without faking it in untyped payloads. I ranked 10 options across all four categories. The highest-leverage cluster is: (1) a thin Economy spike to empirically flush blockers, (2) a generic scalar-attribute subsystem generalized from MoodModifier (one primitive backs Economy/Combat/Politics/Survival at once), and (3) the requirement-generalization + capability-registry hardening the spike forces. The single recommendation is to run the spike FIRST, then build the scalar subsystem against the real blockers it exposes — proving generality before abstracting it."
}
